# What must never enter the image.
#
# This file exists twice, byte for byte: podman reads `.containerignore` and
# Docker BuildKit reads only `.dockerignore`, and `compose.yaml` offers
# `docker compose up -d` as an equivalent command. With only one of them present,
# a build with the other tool does `COPY . .` over everything below — including
# `.env`, which would bake the bot token, the Discord client secret and the
# Twitch credentials into an image layer where a later `RUN rm` cannot reach
# them. That is the failure mode that succeeds rather than erroring, so
# `tests/test_configuration_security.py` asserts the two files stay identical.
#
# The build does `COPY . .`, so anything left lying in a working tree would
# otherwise be baked in — the same reason the publisher derives its snapshot
# from `git ls-files` rather than from disk.
.git
.env
*.db
*.db-wal
*.db-shm
*.db.backup*
backups/
logs/
venv/
.venv/
.claude/
dashboard-reference/
.local-dev/
# The generated session-signing secret. Baking it in would ship one
# installation's key to every image built from this tree.
.dashboard_session_secret
# Game icons: uploaded to Discord by hand, read by no code, other people's
# artwork. The bot's own avatar lives at the repository root and does ship.
botdata/
# Compiled bytecode is rebuilt in the image and only bloats the layer.
__pycache__/
*.pyc
